/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-188-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-189-### Small Group Sizes
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-190-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd:191:Small group sizes can sometimes cause estimation problems in the `did` package.  If a group-time average treatment effect cannot be estimated (e.g., because a group has too few observations relative to the number of covariates in the model, the covariate matrix for some comparison is singular, or there are overlap violations), `att_gt` issues a warning for the affected $(g,t)$ cell and reports `NA` for that $ATT(g,t)$ rather than erroring.  (The exception is when the never-treated comparison group itself is too small, in which case `att_gt` stops and suggests setting `control_group = "notyettreated"`.)  The `did` package also reports a warning if any group has fewer observations than the number of covariates in the model plus five.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-192-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-193-In addition, statistical inference, particularly on group-time average treatment may become more tenuous with small groups.  For example, the effective sample size for estimating the change in outcomes over time for individuals in a particular group is equal to the number of observations in that group and asymptotic results are unlikely to provide good approximations to the sampling distribution of group-time average treatment effects when the number of units in a group is small. In these cases, one should be very cautious about interpreting the $ATT(g,t)$ results for these small groups.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-194-
--
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-196-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-197-### Selecting Alternative Control Groups
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-198-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd:199:By default, the `did` package uses the group of units that never participate in the treatment as the control group.  In this case, if there is no group that never participates, then the `did` package will drop the last period and set units that do not become treated until the last period as the control group (this will also throw a warning).  The other option for the control group is to use the "not yet treated".  The "not yet treated" include the never treated as well as those units that, for a particular point in time, have not been treated yet (though they eventually become treated).  This group is at least as large as the never treated group though it changes across time periods.  To use the "not yet treated" as the control, set the option `control_group="notyettreated"`.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-200-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-201-```{r eval=FALSE}
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd:202:example_attgt_altcontrol <- att_gt(yname = "Y",
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-203-                                   tname = "period",
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-204-                                   idname = "id",
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-205-                                   gname = "G",
--
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-223-The `did` package implements all the $2 \times 2$ DiD estimators that are in the `DRDID` package.  By default, the `did` package uses "doubly robust" estimators that are based on first step linear regressions for the outcome variable and logit for the generalized propensity score.  The other options are "ipw" for inverse probability weighting and "reg" for regression.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/did-basics.Rmd-224-
