/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd-122-* $G_i$ is the time period when unit $i$ becomes treated (often *groups* are defined by the time period when a unit becomes treated; hence, the $G$ notation).
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd-123-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd:124:* $C_i$ is an indicator variable for whether unit $i$ is in a **never-treated** group.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd-125-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd-126-* $D_{it}$ is an indicator variable for whether unit $i$ has been treated by time $t$.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd-127-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd:128:* $Y_{it}$ is unit $i$'s observed outcome in time period $t$.  For units in the never-treated group, $Y_{it} = Y_{it}(0)$ in all time periods.  For units in other groups, we observe $Y_{it} = \mathbf{1}\{ G_i > t\} Y_{it}(0) + \mathbf{1}\{G_i \leq t \} Y_{it}(G_i)$.  The notation here is a bit complicated, but in words, we observe untreated potential outcomes for units that have not yet participated in the treatment, and we observe treated potential outcomes for units once they start to participate in the treatment (and these can depend on *when* they became treated). Implicit in this notation there is a **no treatment anticipation** assumption, which can be relaxed as discussed in [Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021), "Difference-in-Differences with Multiple Time Periods"](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeconom.2020.12.001).
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd-129-
