/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-392-## Setup with a group that remains untreated at the end of the sample
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-393-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:394:One of the features of the above DGP is that all units eventually get treated. In some applications, we have that a group of units remain untreated at the end of the sample. We call this group of units the "never-treated" group. To assess if the problems associated with TWFE specification highlighted above persist if such a group of units is available, we modify a little bit the DGP and repeat the same exercise as above.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-395-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-396-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:397:### DGP with a "never-treated" group and homogeneous treatment effect dynamics
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-398-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:399:The DGP we consider is very similar to the one we have already considered. The only difference is that now we suppress treatment for the last cohort. Put it simply, the cohort with $g=2004$ is now "never-treated", implying that, in this DGP the unit-specific treatment effects at time $t$  $\tau_{i,t}$ are generated as 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-400-$$ \tau_{i,t} = \mu \times (t - g + 1)\times 1\{t \ge g \}1\{g\not=2004 \},$$
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-401-where $\mu$ is the the instantaneous treatment effect; we set $\mu=1$.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-402-
--
