--
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-669-Next, we show that the procedure put forward by Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021) and implemented in our **did** can also be used in this setup.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-670-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-671-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:672:In this specific DGP, as there is a "never-treated" group, we can use use different comparison groups: 1) the "never-treated" group, and 2) the "not-yet-treated" group. In this particular DGP, using any of these two different comparison groups should recover the true underlying dynamic treatment effect. Let's see if this is indeed the case.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-673-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-674-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:675:First, let's estimate it using the "never-treated" units as the comparison group.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-676-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-677-```{r CS_never, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE, cache = TRUE, cache.lazy = TRUE, fig.width=8,fig.height=5, fig.align='center', out.width="90%", dpi = 200}
