/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-25-In this vignette, we want to illustrate the dangers of relying on traditional two-way fixed-effects (TWFE) regression models with leads and lags to conduct causal inference about treatment effect dynamics. In fact, we will show that *even* when treatment effect dynamics are homogeneous across different treatment groups/cohorts, some of these TWFE regression models can be severely biased. 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-26-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-27-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-28-In order to effectively communicate these dangers, we consider a stylized simulation example where covariates do not play any major role. This example draws from several discussions we have had with Andrew Baker. We consider three setups. In the first one all units are eventually treated, and treatment effect dynamics are homogeneous across treatment groups. In the second, we have a group of units that remain untreated at the end of our sample, and treatment effect dynamics are homogeneous across treatment groups. The third one is like the second, but with heterogeneous treatment effect dynamics across treatment groups.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-29-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-30-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-31-## Setup with all units being eventually treated and homogeneous treatment effect dynamics
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-32-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:33:Consider a staggered treatment adoption setup where 40 'states' ($state = \{1,2,\dots,40\}$) are randomly assigned into 4 treatment groups depending on the treatment starting year (1986, 1992, 1998, and 2004). We denote the treatment starting period as $g$ with ($g \in \{ 1986, 1992, 1998, 2004\}$). The 1000 units (e.g., 'counties' or 'firms') $i$ in the sample are randomly assigned to one of the 40 states. Let $G_i$ indicates the group/cohort unit $i$ belongs to, i.e., $G \subseteq \{ 1986, 1992, 1998, 2004\}$.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-34-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-35-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-36-The data generating process (DGP) for the outcome $Y$ we consider is 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-37-$$Y_{i,t} = (2010-g) + \alpha_i + \alpha_t + \tau_{i,t} + \epsilon_{i,t}$$
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-38-where $\alpha_i$ are unit fixed effects drawn from $\sim N(\mu_{state}, 1)$ with state-specific mean $\mu_{state} = state/5$, $\alpha_t$ are time fixed effects (cohort specific parallel time-trends) generated as $$\alpha_t = 0.1 * (t - g) + \epsilon^{time FE}_t, $$ with $\epsilon^{time FE}_t \sim N(0, 1)$, $\epsilon_{i,t} \sim N\left(0, \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^2\right)$ is an idiosyncratic error term, and $\tau_{i,t}$ are the unit-specific treatment effects at time $t$ generated as 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-39-$$ \tau_{i,t} = \mu \times (t - g + 1)\times 1\{t \ge g \},$$
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-40-where $\mu$ is the the instantaneous treatment effect; we set $\mu=1$.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-41-
--
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-55-theme_set(theme_clean() + theme(plot.background = element_blank()))
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-56-#----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-57-iseed  = 20201221
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-58-nrep <- 100  
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-59-true_mu <- 1
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-60-set.seed(iseed)
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-61-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-62-#----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:63:## Generate data - treated cohorts consist of 250 obs each, with the treatment effect still = true_mu on average
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-64-make_data <- function(nobs = 1000, 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-65-                      nstates = 40) {
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-66-  
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-67-  # unit fixed effects (unobserved heterogeneity)
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-68-  unit <- tibble(
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-69-    unit = 1:nobs,
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-70-    # generate state
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-71-    state = sample(1:nstates, nobs, replace = TRUE),
--
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-93-  # full interaction of unit X year 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-94-  expand_grid(unit = 1:nobs, year = 1980:2010) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-95-    left_join(., unit) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-96-    left_join(., year) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-97-    left_join(., treat_taus) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-98-    # make error term and get treatment indicators and treatment effects
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-99-    # Also get cohort specific trends (modify time FE)
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-100-    mutate(error = rnorm(nobs*31, 0, 1),
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:101:           treat = ifelse(year >= cohort_year, 1, 0),
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:102:           tau = ifelse(treat == 1, mu, 0),
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-103-           year_fe = year_fe + 0.1*(year - cohort_year)
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-104-    ) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-105-    # calculate cumulative treatment effects
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-106-    group_by(unit) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-107-    mutate(tau_cum = cumsum(tau)) %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-108-    ungroup() %>% 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-109-    # calculate the dep variable
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-110-    mutate(dep_var = (2010 - cohort_year) + unit_fe + year_fe + tau_cum + error)
--
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-145-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-146-The above plot shows the 1,000 individual values of $Y_{i,t}$, as well as the average by treatment-group (the thicker colorful lines), and the vertical lines show the period when treatment begins for each group. Because treatment effects here grows linearly with time elapsed since treatment started, we can see that the earliest treated units end up with the highest value of Y, and that the difference grows by the gap between treatment years.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-147-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-148-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-149-### Estimating dynamic treatment effects via TWFE event-study regressions
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-150-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-151-Given that we are interested in treatment effect dynamics, we then proceed to consider a classical two-way fixed-effects (TWFE) event study specification 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-152-$$Y_{i,t} = \alpha_i + \alpha_t + \gamma_k^{-K}~ D_{i,t}^{<-K} + \sum_{k=-K}^{-2} \gamma_k^{lead} D_{i,t}^{k} +\sum_{k=0}^{L} \gamma_k^{lag} D_{i,t}^{k} + \gamma_k^{L+} D_{i,t}^{>L} + \varepsilon_{i,t}$$ 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:153:where $D_{i,t}^{k}=1\{t-G_i=k\}$ is an "event-study" dummy variable that takes value one if a unit $i$ is $k$ periods away from initial treatment at time $t$ and zero otherwise, $D_{i,t}^{<-K}=1\{t-G_i<-K\}$ and $D_{i,t}^{>L}=1\{t-G_i>L\}$ are defined analogously. For instance, $D_{i,t}^{0}$ is equal to one if the unit $i$ is first treated at time $t$, $D_{i,t}^{1}$ is equal to one if a one period has passed since treatment started (treatment lags), etc. Alternatively we have that $D_{i,t}^{-2}$ is equal to one if a unit $i$ will be treated in two periods from $t$ (treatment leads). In this exercise we set $K$ and $L$ to be equal to 5.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-154-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-155-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-156-Up to today, it is customary to interpret estimates of $\gamma_k^{lags}$ as ``good'' measures of the average treatment effect for being exposed to treatment for $k$ periods, and estimates of $\gamma_k^{leads}$ as measures of pre-trends. Our first exercise here is to assess if this is OK-ish. 
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-157-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-158-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-159-Towards this end, we will a perform Monte Carlo exercise where we generate data according to the DGP described above and estimate all the parameters of the TWFE event-study regression above. We repeat this 100 times (same holds true if we do 1,000) and plot the Monte Carlo mean and 1.96 times the Monte-Carlo standard-deviation of the relative time indicator dummies, as well as the true treatment effect below.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-160-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-161-
