/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd:237:The results above show that these TWFE event-study type estimates are *severely biased* for the true treatment effects. Furthermore, using the estimates of coefficient of treatment leads as a way to find evidence of "pre-trends" is very problematic, as illustrated above; see Sun and Abraham (2021) for formal results! Putting it simply, the results above highlight that such TWFE linear regression should not be used to highlight treatment effect dynamics!
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-238-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-239-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-240-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-241-Now, you may wonder: What happens if I include all possible leads and lags in the TWFE event study specification, i.e., to set K and L to the maximum allowable in the data, making inclusion of $D_{i,t}^{<-K}$
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-242-and of $D_{i,t}^{>L}$ unnecessary. In what follows, we do exactly that, though we need to omit one extra period apart from -1 to avoid problems of multicollinearity identified by Borusyak and Jaravel (2018). We drop the most negative time period, which is perhaps the most natural thing to do. In order words, we set $K=23$ and $L=24$, and drop the  $D_{i,t}^{<-K}$ and $D_{i,t}^{>L}$ from the TWFE specification.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-243-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-244-
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/TWFE.Rmd-245-The result of this exercise is displayed below. And, as you can see, this still leads to *very biased* estimates for the causal effects of interest!
--
